at was her tone of voice and her  demeanor like during these conversations  maureen wanted to go through with the  divorce but she was worried about how it  would impact her children  that was her main primary focus she  didn't want  the children to have to go through it  and for the children to be affected by  it in any way and that was  one of the reasons that was her reason  for staying and trying to make it work  constantly  a friend of bill argy also took the  stand testifying he spoke to the  defendant just days before his wife's  death  he was not doing good he was in a bad  place  he was very angry  and sad  at the same time  do you recall him saying anything about  his job status  at that time i do recall him saying he  didn't have a job  what do you recall him saying about the  status of his marriage  that it was crumbling  that maureen  was asking for a divorce  do you recall him discussing bankruptcy  i do and what did he say about that that  the house is being repossessed  that they  they don't have anything  here to discuss the latest in the new  hampshire murder trial for insurance is  civil rights attorney katie smith and  co-host terry austin terry maureen the  victim in this case obviously can't take  the stand but to what her brother say  bring the trial a little bit closer to  her prosecution on her behalf  actually maureen's brother matthew was  an excellent witness he served two  purposes first of all they wanted to  bring her to life and he talked about  the fact that she was soft-spoken she  was a great mom she was caring for other  people and then they also wanted to  establish that she never wanted to  commit suicide and they did that by  showing the emails prior to her death  she was coordinating the fact that she  was making a move and the move was  scheduled for april 6th and 7th so she  basically was trying to live and moving  forward with her life  now on the other side katie how  incriminating are argie's friend's words  uh in testifying about his kind of  mentality before the death does this  almost mean that rg has to testify to  try to explain this away  i mean brian it's not good i mean of  course one of the things that the  prosecution always wants to show but  it's not required is motive and there's  so much motive here i mean there's 400  000 some motivating factors with the  money we know that he was in a desperate  place and his friends really paint him  in that exact spot so it's problematic i  think the best thing the defense has is  to really show that this was not a  homicide and it was a suicide but that  again is also a very uphill battle  absolutely uphill battle for william rg  indeed it's going to be interesting with  all of the circumstantial evidence all  of this mode of what the defense can do  because right now seems like a very  strong case for the prosecution  when we come back what we know so far  about the 15 year old suspect in the  michigan high school shooting what led  up to the deadly attack and what charges  he now faces more on the investigation  next  welcome back to law and crime daily  investigators say the 15-year-old boy  responsible for the deadly school  shooting in michigan met with school  officials just hours before a shooting  about concerning behavior  on wednesday prosecutors announced ethan  crumbly will be charged as an adult on  multiple accounts including terrorism  causing death first degree murder and  assault with intent to murder this comes  after tuesday's deadly shooting in  oxford michigan where officials say  crumbly shot and killed four people  seven more were wounded prosecutors say  he methodically and deliberately shot at  students and teachers firing 30 rounds  during a 5-minute period officials later  found cell phone video and a journal  entries detailing his plans to kill  fellow classmates on wednesday crumbly  was arraigned in oakland county he  refused to speak during his appearance  but pled not guilty through his attorney  right now authorities are still working  to determine why the suspect planned the  attack  any updates on a motive just yet sure  no  we obviously are not communicating with  the suspect so we can't get that from  him we're working you know items that we  have seized as i talked about documents  papers phones evidentiary value items to  look to see if there's something that  would lead to  us understanding what motivate him but i  will say again there is nothing  that he could have  faced that would warrant  senseless  absolutely brutal violence on other kids  and i have also asked the school if they  had any records of him being bullied and  the coordinator of anti-bullying  programs  had no information that he'd been  bullied by anyone  katie journals and cell phone video how  will that affect uh this criminal case  against ethan crumbly  it's not good i mean the worst words  that could be used against you are your  own and having some sort of written  documentation of your motive that  actually coincides with the acts that  you took are extremely damaging and i'm  certain that they would be admissible  and relevant if they really did mirror  what actually occurred  now terry the video that has gone out of  the  students inside the michigan school  hearing that knock and hearing someone  say come out and check my badge bro we  found out that's an actual sheriff but  one thing that i think should be  applauded and is also a little  heartbreaking is the quick thinking of  those students to recognize a word such  as bro which i was bizarre to me that an  officer is using that word and then  react in the way that they did what was  your reaction to the offers to the  students sorry quick thinking well you  know it is a good thing that it turned  out to actually be the officer who was  trying to get in but you also have to  applaud those students it was brilliant  of them to be thinking about the fact  that the shooter could  have been trying to disguise himself as  a police officer and so they would not  open that door unless they were 100 sure  the reason that it's heartbreaking is  because our students today have to be  trained on how to avoid these types of  situations how to get out of these types  of situations and these students were  thinking very quickly on their feet they  went in the room they hid they you know  bundled together and then when they had  some doubt as to whether or not this was  a shooter or whether it was a police  officer they aired on the side of  caution and they fled and they fled into  the arms of real police officers and  again of course that was a real police  officer but good thinking on their part  absolutely and thank you very much katie  as well as terry and thank you for  joining us here on law and crime daily  we'll see you next time as we discuss  justice in america  [Music]  you
 

